      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Acids</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">bases</span>, and <span class="learn-keyword">salts</span> are fundamental classes of chemical compounds. They react in neutralisation reactions to form salts and water.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Definitions</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Acid</th><th>Base</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arrhenius</td><td>Produces H⁺ in water</td><td>Produces OH⁻ in water</td></tr><tr><td>Brønsted–Lowry</td><td>H⁺ donor</td><td>H⁺ acceptor</td></tr><tr><td>Lewis</td><td>Electron pair acceptor</td><td>Electron pair donor</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Properties</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li>Acids: pH &lt; 7; turn blue litmus red; react with metals (H₂↑), carbonates (CO₂↑), bases (neutralisation)</li><li>Strong acids (HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃) fully dissociate; weak acids (CH₃COOH) partially dissociate</li><li>Bases: pH &gt; 7; turn red litmus blue; alkali = soluble base</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. pH Scale</h3><p class="learn-p">pH = −log[H⁺]. Scale 0–14: 0–6 acidic; 7 neutral; 8–14 alkaline.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Salt Preparation</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Acid + Metal</td><td>Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂</td></tr><tr><td>Acid + Metal oxide</td><td>CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O</td></tr><tr><td>Acid + Carbonate</td><td>CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂</td></tr><tr><td>Neutralisation</td><td>NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O</td></tr><tr><td>Precipitation</td><td>BaCl₂ + Na₂SO₄ → BaSO₄↓ + 2NaCl</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Key:</strong> Strong acid ≠ concentrated acid. Strong = fully dissociates. Weak = partially. Insoluble salts made by precipitation.</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Arrhenius: H⁺/OH⁻. Brønsted–Lowry: H⁺ donor/acceptor. Lewis: e⁻ pair acceptor/donor. pH&lt;7=acid; 7=neutral; &gt;7=alkaline. Insoluble salts by precipitation; soluble by neutralisation.</div>',
