      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Solubility</span> is the maximum mass of a solute that dissolves in 100 g of solvent at a given temperature to form a saturated solution. <span class="learn-keyword">Solubility curves</span> show how solubility changes with temperature.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Key Definitions</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>Saturated solution:</strong> cannot dissolve more solute at that temperature</li><li><strong>Unsaturated solution:</strong> can dissolve more solute</li><li><strong>Supersaturated solution:</strong> unstable; more dissolved than normally possible; crystallises on disturbance</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Effect of Temperature on Solubility</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li>Most solids: solubility increases with temperature (KNO₃, KCl, NaCl)</li><li>Gases: solubility decreases with increasing temperature (CO₂ in water)</li><li>Exceptions: CaSO₄ — solubility decreases with temperature (exothermic dissolution)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Crystal Calculation</h3><p class="learn-p">Mass of crystals = (solubility at higher T − solubility at lower T) × (mass of water / 100).</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Factors Affecting Solubility</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li>Temperature (most important for solids)</li><li>Pressure (affects gases — Henry\'s Law: S ∝ P)</li><li>Nature of solute and solvent (like dissolves like)</li></ul><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Crystal Calculation:</strong> Mass of crystals = difference in solubility × (water mass / 100).</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Solubility = g solute per 100 g water at given T. Saturated = maximum dissolved. Solid solubility mostly ↑ with T; gas solubility ↓ with T. Henry\'s Law: gas solubility ∝ pressure.</div>',
