      '<div class="learn-intro">All matter is made of <span class="learn-keyword">atoms</span>. Understanding atomic structure explains chemical properties, bonding, and the periodic table.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Subatomic Particles</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Particle</th><th>Charge</th><th>Mass</th><th>Location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Proton (p⁺)</td><td>+1</td><td>1</td><td>Nucleus</td></tr><tr><td>Neutron (n⁰)</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>Nucleus</td></tr><tr><td>Electron (e⁻)</td><td>−1</td><td>≈0 (1/1836)</td><td>Shells/orbitals</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Atomic Number and Mass Number</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>Z (atomic number):</strong> number of protons — defines the element</li><li><strong>A (mass number):</strong> protons + neutrons</li><li><strong>Neutrons:</strong> N = A − Z</li><li>Neutral atom: electrons = protons = Z</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Isotopes</h3><p class="learn-p">Same Z, different A (different neutrons). Same chemical properties; slightly different physical properties. Examples: ³⁵Cl (75%) and ³⁷Cl (25%) → Ar = 35.5</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Electronic Configuration</h3><p class="learn-p">Max electrons per shell: 2n². Shell 1=2, Shell 2=8, Shell 3=18. Examples: Na(Z=11): 2,8,1. Ca(Z=20): 2,8,8,2.</p><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Relative Atomic Mass:</strong> Ar = weighted average. Cl: (35×75 + 37×25)/100 = 35.5.</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Proton (Z, +1, nucleus). Neutron (N=A−Z, neutral). Electron (≈0, −1, shells). Isotopes: same Z, different A. Config: 2,8,18... Ar = weighted average.</div>',
