      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Chemical bonds</span> hold atoms together. Atoms bond to achieve stable noble gas configurations. Bond type depends on the nature of atoms and electronegativity differences.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Ionic Bonding</h3><p class="learn-p">Electrons transferred from metal to non-metal → ions attract electrostatically. High MP/BP; conduct when dissolved/molten; soluble in water; brittle.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Covalent Bonding</h3><p class="learn-p">Electrons shared between non-metals. Single (1 pair), double (2 pairs), triple (3 pairs). Low MP/BP; poor conductors; often insoluble in water. Dative bond: both electrons from one atom.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Metallic Bonding</h3><p class="learn-p">Sea of delocalised electrons around positive metal ions. Explains conductivity, malleability, ductility, lustre.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Electronegativity</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>EN Difference</th><th>Bond Type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>Pure covalent</td></tr><tr><td>0.1–1.7</td><td>Polar covalent</td></tr><tr><td>&gt;1.7</td><td>Ionic</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">5. VSEPR Shapes</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Molecule</th><th>Bonding Pairs</th><th>Lone Pairs</th><th>Shape</th><th>Angle</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>BeCl₂</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>Linear</td><td>180°</td></tr><tr><td>BF₃</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>Trigonal planar</td><td>120°</td></tr><tr><td>CH₄</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>Tetrahedral</td><td>109.5°</td></tr><tr><td>NH₃</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>Pyramidal</td><td>107°</td></tr><tr><td>H₂O</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>Bent</td><td>104.5°</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>VSEPR:</strong> Lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs. CH₄=109.5°, NH₃=107°, H₂O=104.5°. Each lone pair reduces bond angle.</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Ionic = electron transfer. Covalent = sharing. Metallic = electron sea. Dative = both electrons from one atom. Lone pairs reduce bond angles.</div>',
