      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Stoichiometry</span> is the quantitative study of chemical reactions using balanced equations to calculate amounts of reactants and products.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Mole Concept</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li>1 mol = 6.02 × 10²³ particles (Avogadro\'s number)</li><li>n = m/M (moles = mass ÷ molar mass)</li><li>At STP: 1 mol gas = 22.4 L</li><li>Concentration c = n/V (mol/L)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Reactions</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Synthesis</td><td>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</td></tr><tr><td>Decomposition</td><td>2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂</td></tr><tr><td>Displacement</td><td>Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu</td></tr><tr><td>Double decomposition</td><td>AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl↓ + NaNO₃</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Stoichiometric Calculations</h3><ol class="learn-list"><li>Write and balance equation</li><li>n = m/M for given substance</li><li>Use mole ratio from equation</li><li>m = n × M for required substance</li></ol><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Limiting Reagent and % Yield</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li>Limiting reagent = completely consumed first; limits product</li><li>% yield = (actual/theoretical) × 100</li></ul><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Key formulae:</strong> n=m/M. n=V/22.4 (L at STP). c=n/V (mol/L). N=n×6.02×10²³.</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> 1 mol = 6.02×10²³ particles. n=m/M. 1 mol gas = 22.4 L at STP. Mole ratio from balanced equation. Limiting reagent consumed first. % yield = (actual/theoretical) × 100.</div>',
