    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '<div class="learn-intro">The <span class="learn-keyword">gas laws</span> describe how the pressure, volume, and temperature of a gas are related. They apply to ideal gases — gases that have no intermolecular forces and whose molecules have negligible volume. Real gases approximate ideal behaviour at low pressures and high temperatures.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Boyle\'s Law (1662)</h3><p class="learn-p">At constant temperature, the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume:</p><p class="learn-p"><strong>P ∝ 1/V (constant T and n) → PV = constant → P₁V₁ = P₂V₂</strong></p><ul class="learn-list"><li>If pressure doubles, volume halves (at constant T).</li><li>Graph of P vs 1/V: straight line through origin.</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Charles\' Law (1787)</h3><p class="learn-p">At constant pressure, the volume of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature (in Kelvin):</p><p class="learn-p"><strong>V ∝ T (constant P and n) → V/T = constant → V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂</strong></p><ul class="learn-list"><li>T must always be in Kelvin: K = °C + 273</li><li>Gas volume → 0 as T → 0 K (absolute zero = −273°C)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Pressure Law (Gay-Lussac\'s Law)</h3><p class="learn-p">At constant volume, pressure is directly proportional to absolute temperature:</p><p class="learn-p"><strong>P/T = constant → P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂</strong></p><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Combined Gas Law</h3><p class="learn-p"><strong>P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂</strong></p><p class="learn-p">This combines Boyle\'s and Charles\' Laws and applies when all three variables change.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Ideal Gas Equation</h3><p class="learn-p"><strong>PV = nRT</strong></p><ul class="learn-list"><li>P = pressure (Pa or atm)</li><li>V = volume (m³ or L)</li><li>n = moles of gas</li><li>R = gas constant = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ (or 0.0821 L atm mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)</li><li>T = absolute temperature (K)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">6. Avogadro\'s Law</h3><p class="learn-p">Equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules. At STP: 1 mole of any gas occupies 22.4 L.</p><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>Variables Constant</th><th>Relationship</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Boyle\'s</td><td>T, n</td><td>PV = k; P₁V₁ = P₂V₂</td></tr><tr><td>Charles\'</td><td>P, n</td><td>V/T = k; V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂</td></tr><tr><td>Gay-Lussac\'s</td><td>V, n</td><td>P/T = k; P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂</td></tr><tr><td>Combined</td><td>n</td><td>P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂</td></tr><tr><td>Ideal gas</td><td>—</td><td>PV = nRT</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Always convert to Kelvin:</strong> T(K) = T(°C) + 273. Forgetting this is the most common gas law mistake. At STP: T = 273 K, P = 101,325 Pa (1 atm).</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Boyle: P₁V₁=P₂V₂ (constant T). Charles: V₁/T₁=V₂/T₂ (constant P). Combined: P₁V₁/T₁=P₂V₂/T₂. Ideal: PV=nRT. Avogadro: 1 mol gas = 22.4 L at STP. Always use Kelvin.</div>',
