    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Electrochemistry</span> is the branch of chemistry that deals with the relationship between chemical reactions and electrical energy. It includes electrolysis (using electricity to drive chemical reactions) and electrochemical cells (using chemical reactions to produce electricity).</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Electrolysis</h3><p class="learn-p">Electrolysis uses direct current (DC) to decompose an electrolyte (ionic compound in solution or molten). Key components:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>Electrolyte:</strong> ionic compound that conducts when dissolved or molten</li><li><strong>Cathode:</strong> negative electrode — cations (positive ions) move here and are reduced (gain electrons)</li><li><strong>Anode:</strong> positive electrode — anions (negative ions) move here and are oxidised (lose electrons)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Electrolysis of Key Electrolytes</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Electrolyte</th><th>Cathode Product</th><th>Anode Product</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dilute H₂SO₄</td><td>H₂ gas</td><td>O₂ gas</td></tr><tr><td>Dilute HCl</td><td>H₂ gas</td><td>Cl₂ gas (or O₂ if very dilute)</td></tr><tr><td>Molten NaCl</td><td>Na metal</td><td>Cl₂ gas</td></tr><tr><td>CuSO₄ (Cu electrodes)</td><td>Cu deposited</td><td>Cu dissolves</td></tr><tr><td>CuSO₄ (Pt electrodes)</td><td>Cu deposited</td><td>O₂ gas</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Faraday\'s Laws of Electrolysis</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>First Law:</strong> The mass of substance deposited at an electrode is proportional to the quantity of electricity (charge) passed. m ∝ Q</li><li><strong>Second Law:</strong> When the same quantity of electricity is passed through different electrolytes, the masses deposited are proportional to the equivalent masses (molar mass ÷ valency).</li><li><strong>Key formula:</strong> Q = It (charge = current × time). Mass deposited: m = (M × I × t)/(n × F), where F = Faraday constant = 96,500 C/mol</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Applications of Electrolysis</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li>Electroplating (coating metal with another metal)</li><li>Extraction of metals (Al from Al₂O₃, Na from molten NaCl)</li><li>Purification of copper</li><li>Manufacture of chlorine and NaOH (chlor-alkali industry)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Electrochemical Cells</h3><p class="learn-p">Galvanic/voltaic cells convert chemical energy to electrical energy. The cell with zinc and copper electrodes (Daniell cell): Zn|Zn²⁺||Cu²⁺|Cu. Anode: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ (oxidation). Cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu (reduction).</p><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>OILRIG:</strong> Oxidation Is Loss (of electrons); Reduction Is Gain (of electrons). At cathode: reduction. At anode: oxidation. Cations go to cathode; anions go to anode.</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Electrolysis: cathode = reduction (cations); anode = oxidation (anions). Faraday: Q=It; m=(MIt)/(nF); F=96,500 C/mol. Applications: electroplating, metal extraction, chlor-alkali. Galvanic cells: chemical → electrical energy.</div>',
