    contentHTML:
      '<div class="learn-intro">The <span class="learn-keyword">rate of a chemical reaction</span> is the speed at which reactants are converted to products — how fast the reaction proceeds. Understanding reaction rates is crucial in industrial chemistry (maximising product yield efficiently) and everyday life (controlling corrosion, food preservation, drug activity).</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Definition and Measurement of Rate</h3><p class="learn-p">Rate = change in concentration / change in time = Δ[concentration] / Δt. Can be measured by monitoring: gas volume evolved, change in mass (gas escaping), change in colour, turbidity (precipitate formation), or electrical conductivity.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Factors Affecting Rate of Reaction</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect on Rate</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Concentration</td><td>Higher concentration → faster rate</td><td>More particles per unit volume → more frequent collisions</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Higher temperature → faster rate</td><td>More particles have energy ≥ Eₐ; more frequent and energetic collisions</td></tr><tr><td>Surface area</td><td>Larger surface area → faster rate</td><td>More surface exposed → more collision sites for reactant particles</td></tr><tr><td>Catalyst</td><td>Catalyst → faster rate</td><td>Lowers activation energy (Eₐ); more particles have enough energy</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure (gases)</td><td>Higher pressure → faster rate</td><td>Gas molecules closer together → more frequent collisions</td></tr><tr><td>Light</td><td>Light increases rate in photochemical reactions</td><td>Provides energy to initiate reaction (e.g., H₂ + Cl₂ in UV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Collision Theory</h3><p class="learn-p">For a reaction to occur, reacting particles must: (1) collide with each other, (2) collide with sufficient energy (≥ activation energy), and (3) collide in the correct orientation. Factors that increase the rate of successful collisions increase the reaction rate.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Maxwell-Boltzmann Distribution</h3><p class="learn-p">The Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution shows the spread of kinetic energies among gas molecules at a given temperature. At higher temperatures, the peak shifts to higher energy and more molecules exceed the activation energy threshold.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Effect of a Catalyst</h3><p class="learn-p">A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with lower Eₐ. On the Maxwell-Boltzmann curve, the vertical line representing Eₐ moves to the left — a greater proportion of molecules now have energy ≥ new lower Eₐ. Rate increases without changing ΔH.</p><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Temperature Rule:</strong> A 10°C rise in temperature approximately doubles the reaction rate. This rule of thumb reflects the exponential dependence of rate on temperature (Arrhenius equation: k = Ae^(−Eₐ/RT)).</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Rate affected by: concentration, temperature, surface area, catalyst, pressure (gases), light. Collision theory: successful collision needs correct energy (≥Eₐ) and orientation. Catalyst: lowers Eₐ, increases rate, does not change ΔH. Temperature rise of 10°C ≈ doubles rate.</div>',
