    contentHTML:
      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Organic chemistry</span> is the study of carbon-containing compounds. Carbon\'s unique ability to form four covalent bonds and to bond to itself in chains and rings makes possible an enormous variety of organic molecules — essential for life, industry, and medicine.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Why Carbon is Special</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li>Forms 4 covalent bonds (tetravalent)</li><li>Can bond to itself in long chains (catenation)</li><li>Forms single, double, and triple bonds</li><li>Stable C–C bonds — great diversity of structures</li><li>Can form ring structures (cyclic compounds)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Homologous Series</h3><p class="learn-p">A homologous series is a family of organic compounds that: (a) have the same general formula; (b) differ by CH₂ from one member to the next; (c) show a gradual change in physical properties (boiling point, density) with increasing chain length; (d) have similar chemical properties.</p><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Series</th><th>Functional Group</th><th>General Formula</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Alkanes</td><td>C–C single bonds only</td><td>CₙH₂ₙ₊₂</td><td>CH₄, C₂H₆, C₃H₈</td></tr><tr><td>Alkenes</td><td>C=C double bond</td><td>CₙH₂ₙ</td><td>C₂H₄, C₃H₆</td></tr><tr><td>Alkynes</td><td>C≡C triple bond</td><td>CₙH₂ₙ₋₂</td><td>C₂H₂ (ethyne)</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohols</td><td>–OH hydroxyl</td><td>CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH</td><td>CH₃OH, C₂H₅OH</td></tr><tr><td>Carboxylic acids</td><td>–COOH</td><td>CₙH₂ₙ₊₁COOH</td><td>CH₃COOH (ethanoic acid)</td></tr><tr><td>Aldehydes</td><td>–CHO</td><td>CₙH₂ₙO</td><td>HCHO (methanal)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. IUPAC Nomenclature</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li>Count the longest carbon chain → prefix (meth-1, eth-2, prop-3, but-4, pent-5, hex-6)</li><li>Identify and name the functional group → suffix (-ane, -ene, -ol, -al, -anoic acid)</li><li>Number the chain from the end nearest the functional group</li><li>Name and number substituents (methyl-, ethyl-)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Structural Isomerism</h3><p class="learn-p">Isomers have the same molecular formula but different structural arrangements. Types: chain isomerism (different carbon skeletons), position isomerism (functional group at different positions), functional group isomerism (same formula, different functional groups).</p><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Prefixes:</strong> meth(1), eth(2), prop(3), but(4), pent(5), hex(6), hept(7), oct(8). Saturated = alkanes (single bonds only). Unsaturated = alkenes/alkynes (double/triple bonds).</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Carbon: tetravalent, catenation. Homologous series: same formula, differ by CH₂, same functional group. Alkanes CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, alkenes CₙH₂ₙ, alkynes CₙH₂ₙ₋₂, alcohols CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH. IUPAC: longest chain + functional group suffix. Isomers: same molecular formula, different structures.</div>',
