    contentHTML:
      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Petroleum</span> (crude oil) is a naturally occurring mixture of hydrocarbons formed over millions of years from the remains of marine organisms under heat and pressure. It is Nigeria\'s most important natural resource and the basis of a vast petrochemical industry. Understanding its refining and uses is essential for both chemistry and national development.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Formation and Composition of Crude Oil</h3><p class="learn-p">Crude oil forms from marine organisms buried under sediment over millions of years. Heat and pressure transform them into a complex mixture of mainly alkanes, cycloalkanes, and aromatic hydrocarbons. Natural gas (mainly methane) is often found above oil deposits.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Fractional Distillation of Crude Oil</h3><p class="learn-p">Crude oil is refined by fractional distillation in a fractionating tower. Crude oil is heated and vaporised; vapours rise up the tower and condense at different heights according to their boiling points.</p><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Fraction</th><th>Approx. Boiling Range</th><th>Carbon Atoms</th><th>Main Uses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Refinery gas (LPG)</td><td>Below 25°C</td><td>C₁–C₄</td><td>Cooking fuel, heating</td></tr><tr><td>Petrol (gasoline)</td><td>25–75°C</td><td>C₅–C₁₀</td><td>Fuel for cars</td></tr><tr><td>Naphtha</td><td>75–150°C</td><td>C₇–C₁₀</td><td>Chemicals, solvents</td></tr><tr><td>Kerosene (jet fuel)</td><td>150–240°C</td><td>C₁₁–C₁₅</td><td>Aircraft fuel, cooking</td></tr><tr><td>Diesel oil (gas oil)</td><td>240–350°C</td><td>C₁₅–C₂₅</td><td>Diesel engines, heating</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel oil</td><td>350–400°C</td><td>C₂₀–C₇₀</td><td>Ships, power stations</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating oil/wax</td><td>400–500°C</td><td>C₃₀–C₅₀</td><td>Engine oil, candles</td></tr><tr><td>Bitumen (asphalt)</td><td>Above 500°C (residue)</td><td>C₇₀+</td><td>Road surfacing, roofing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Cracking</h3><p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Cracking</span> breaks down large, less useful hydrocarbon molecules into smaller, more useful ones — especially petrol and ethene (for plastics).</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>Thermal cracking:</strong> High temperature (700–900°C) and pressure; produces alkenes and alkanes.</li><li><strong>Catalytic cracking:</strong> Lower temperature (500°C) using zeolite/aluminium oxide catalyst; produces high-octane petrol and alkenes.</li><li>Example: C₁₆H₃₄ → C₈H₁₈ + C₈H₁₆ (alkane + alkene)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Reforming</h3><p class="learn-p">Reforming converts straight-chain alkanes into branched alkanes and aromatic compounds (benzene, toluene) to improve petrol\'s octane rating. Uses platinum or rhenium catalyst.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Petrochemicals</h3><p class="learn-p">Petrochemicals are chemicals derived from petroleum/natural gas, used as raw materials for the chemical industry:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li>Ethene → polyethylene (plastic bags), ethanol, PVC</li><li>Propene → polypropylene, propan-2-ol</li><li>Benzene → drugs, dyes, explosives, plastics</li><li>Methane → ammonia (Haber process), methanol, hydrogen fuel</li></ul><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Cracking Summary:</strong> Large hydrocarbons → smaller (alkenes + alkanes). Alkenes (unsaturated) are needed for making plastics. Cracking produces ethene (C₂H₄) — the most important petrochemical feedstock for the polymer industry.</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Crude oil = mixture of hydrocarbons. Refining = fractional distillation (smallest/lowest BP at top: LPG, petrol, naphtha, kerosene, diesel, fuel oil, bitumen at bottom). Cracking: large → small molecules + alkenes. Reforming: straight chain → branched/aromatic (higher octane). Petrochemicals: ethene, propene, benzene, methane.</div>',
